#Problem APEX 5.5.24

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Techniques of integration)
## DBsection(Approximation)
## KEYWORDS('numerical integration', 'quadrature', "Simpson's Rule")
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## MLT(approx_07)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('5.5')
## Problem1('24')
## RESOURCES('APEX_5.5_24_fig1.png')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGnumericalmacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');
Context('Numeric');

@x = (
    0,
    random(3, 5, 0.1),
    random(3, 5, 0.1),
    random(4, 6, 0.1),
    random(5, 7, 0.1),
    random(5, 7, 0.1), 0
);

$ans1 = Real(
    (
        $x[0] +
            4 * $x[1] +
            2 * $x[2] +
            4 * $x[3] +
            2 * $x[4] +
            4 * $x[5] +
            $x[6]
    ) / 3
);

BEGIN_PGML
A lake is shown below and the distances in the table to its side:

[#
    [.[!Picture of Lake with cross sections labelled A through E!]{'APEX_5.5_24_fig1.png'}{250}.] [.
    [#
        [.A.][.[$x[1]].]*
        [.B.][.[$x[2]].]*
        [.C.][.[$x[3]].]*
        [.D.][.[$x[4]].]*
        [.E.][.[$x[5]].]*
    #]{horizontalrules=>1, align => '|c|c|'}
    .]
#]

>> (_Figure not drawn to scale._) <<

Approximate the area of the lake using Simpson's Rule,
where the measurements (listed in the table below
the figure) are in meters, taken in
1 m increments.

Area [`\approx`][_]{$ans1}{10} m[`{}^2`]
END_PGML

$rows = [ map { [ $_, $x[$_] ] } (0 .. 6) ];

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Use Simpson's Rule with [`n = 6`].

[#
    [.[`i`].][.[`y_i`].]*
    [. .]{rows => $rows}
#]{horizontalrules => 1, align => '|c|c|'}

[```
  \textrm{Area} \approx
  \dfrac{1}{3}\big( [$x[0]] + 4([$x[1]]) + 2([$x[2]]) +
  4([$x[3]]) + 2([$x[4]]) + 4([$x[5]]) + [$x[6]] \big) = [$ans1]
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
